snapctrlalt (1.2.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The end-to-end suite now draws a rectangle with real mouse input and checks
    the exported pixels: the stroke has to land on all four expected edges and
    the interior has to stay untouched.  This closes the last gap in the
    "what you select is what you get" guarantee -- the annotation path was
    previously only covered offscreen.

 -- dsh (deepseek harness) <dsh@localhost>  Fri, 25 Sep 2026 23:59:00 +0800

snapctrlalt (1.2.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed the selection bounds on multi-monitor desktops whose virtual screen
    does not start at (0, 0) -- a monitor placed to the left of or above the
    primary one gives the root window a negative origin.  Every clamp used to
    assume 0 as the lower bound, which would have pushed selections on that
    monitor off by the origin; the bounds now come from the calibrated canvas
    rectangle, and "select all" uses it too.
  * Made bin/snapctrlalt robust when it is reached through a symlink: it now
    looks for the package next to both the link and the resolved path, so a
    link into a source checkout and a ~/.local install both work, while a dpkg
    install still uses the system packages without touching sys.path.
  * packaging/debian/rules now explains what to do instead of failing with a
    bare make error when debhelper is not installed.
  * Test suite grew to 36 checks: virtual screen origins that are not (0, 0),
    and 1.25x / 1.75x fractional scaling with a full-screen sweep asserting no
    accumulated rounding error.

 -- dsh (deepseek harness) <dsh@localhost>  Fri, 25 Sep 2026 23:50:00 +0800

snapctrlalt (1.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Linux port of SnapCtrlAlt: global hotkey, full screen overlay, annotation
    tools, clipboard output, tray resident.
  * Fixed the selection offset on HiDPI displays.  The overlay ran under GDK's
    scaling, so the event coordinates, the window allocation and the captured
    image were derived from different scaling factors; on this machine the
    2880x1800 root window even produced a 5760x3600 capture that was GDK's own
    upscale rather than a real resolution.  The overlay now runs at device
    pixel scale, so the event coordinates, the window, the X11 root window and
    the captured pixels are the same space, and a runtime calibration verifies
    the pointer mapping against the X11 root pointer, correcting any residual
    translation or scaling and logging what it found.
  * The capture path now prefers Xlib, which always returns the root window's
    real pixels, instead of GDK's scaled pixbuf.
  * Added independent UI scaling for the toolbar, magnifier and handles, so
    keeping the coordinates exact no longer makes the interface tiny on HiDPI
    panels.
  * Added tests/test_coords.py: calibration maths across 1x, 1.5x, 2x and 3x,
    pointer self-check behaviour including faulty samples, and a pixel exact
    end-to-end check that the selected region equals the exported image.

 -- dsh (deepseek harness) <dsh@localhost>  Fri, 25 Sep 2026 16:00:00 +0800

